  and because both feed the same store, adding the mutation producer later is additive rather than a rewrite. This does
  mean tasks need a due-date field, which touches the task model you don't have yet.

[38;5;246m⏺ [39mUser answered Claude's questions:
[38;5;246m  ⎿  · What should trigger a notification? → Due/overdue only (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Next: where the notification actually shows up.

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether notifications stay inside the page or escape to the OS.

  - [1mIn-page only[0m — a bell/badge and a dropdown list in the app. Works everywhere, no permissions, no HTTPS requirement,
    fully testable. But it only informs you when you're already looking at the app, which blunts the point of a deadline
    reminder.
  - [1mBrowser Notifications API[0m — real OS-level toasts. Requires an explicit permission prompt, a secure context (HTTPS or
    localhost), and it still only fires while the tab is alive — waking a closed app needs a service worker plus a push
    server, which you've ruled out. Adds a permission-state machine (default/granted/denied) and a denied-path fallback
    you have to build anyway.
  - [1mBoth, in-page as the source of truth[0m — the store and the in-page list are canonical; OS toasts are a best-effort
    mirror layered on top. Costs the permission handling, but degrades cleanly to case one when permission is refused or
    unavailable.

  [1mMy recommendation: both, with in-page as the source of truth.[0m The permission-denied fallback [3mis[0m the in-page list, so
  you build that regardless — the OS layer is a thin, optional addition rather than a parallel system. If you'd rather
  cut scope, in-page only is the clean subset and stays forward-compatible.
